WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.827
Maps, caches, registries, and lookup tables are deceptively difficult under concurrency because they combine several responsibilities.

2
00:00:05.827 --> 00:00:15.296
A single operation may look like a read, but the structure may also refresh timestamps, promote an item in an eviction list, insert a missing value,

3
00:00:15.296 --> 00:00:16.753
or remove stale entries.

4
00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:23.673
The first design question is whether reads are truly read-only and what lifetime guarantee a successful lookup provides.

5
00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:32.778
Returning a direct reference to an internal object can be unsafe if another thread may erase or replace that object immediately after the lock is

6
00:00:32.778 --> 00:00:33.142
released.

7
00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:39.698
Reader-writer locks can help read-heavy workloads, but they do not remove the need for clear invariants.

8
00:00:39.698 --> 00:00:45.889
Shared access is only safe while operations are actually read-only with respect to every protected field.

9
00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:53.174
If a lookup updates metrics, cache ordering, or lazy initialization state, it may require exclusive access or a different design.

10
00:00:53.174 --> 00:01:01.914
Immutable snapshots are often simpler: a reader receives an owned value, a copy of relevant data, or a shared immutable view that remains valid

11
00:01:01.914 --> 00:01:03.371
independent of later mutation.

12
00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:05.921
Caches add resource governance to the problem.

13
00:01:05.921 --> 00:01:10.655
Size limits, eviction rules, stale-entry handling, and refresh behavior should be explicit.

14
00:01:10.655 --> 00:01:18.668
Without those rules, a cache can become an availability risk by growing without bound, serving data beyond its intended lifetime, or destroying

15
00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:21.581
entries while other threads still depend on them.

16
00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.402
Defensive APIs avoid surprising callers.

17
00:01:23.402 --> 00:01:30.686
They document whether returned values are copies, shared handles, scoped access objects, or temporary observations, and they test replacement and

18
00:01:30.686 --> 00:01:33.600
eviction paths as carefully as successful lookup paths.
